{
  "source": "CODEFORCES",
  "id": "2278A",
  "title": "Fraction and Six",
  "description": "<h2>Problem</h2>\n<p>You are given two strings <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span>, consisting of lowercase English letters.</p><p>Interpret the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span> as the product of variables written in the numerator of a fraction, and the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span> as the product of variables written in the denominator. <span class=\"tex-font-style-bf\">Each character represents a variable</span>.</p><p>Since multiplication is commutative, the variables in both the numerator and the denominator may be reordered arbitrarily. In addition, any variable that appears in both the numerator and the denominator may be cancelled. Note that a single occurrence of a variable in the denominator can only cancel exactly one occurrence of that variable in the numerator and vice versa.</p><p>Determine whether it is possible to simplify the fraction so that the resulting expression is exactly equal to the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">six</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\texttt{six}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. In other words, after performing any number of cancellations and rearrangements, the remaining variables in the numerator must form the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">six</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\texttt{six}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and no variables may remain in the denominator.</p><p>For example, if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">sinx</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\texttt{sinx}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">n</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\texttt{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then the variable <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">n</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\texttt{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span> can be cancelled, leaving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">six</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\texttt{six}</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the numerator.</p><center> <img class=\"tex-graphics\" src=\"https://espresso.codeforces.com/1ac4929438e4908210cb2bb8872c7d6702fcd18f.png\" style=\"zoom: 40.0%;max-width: 100.0%;max-height: 100.0%;\">   <span class=\"tex-font-size-small\"></span> </center>\n<h2>Input</h2>\n<p>The first line of the input contains a single integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></span> (<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 \\le t \\le 100</annotation></semantics></math></span>) — the number of test cases. The description of each test case follows.</p><p>The first line of each test case contains two integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> (<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 \\le n, m \\le 100</annotation></semantics></math></span>) — the lengths of the strings <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span>, respectively.</p><p>The second line of each test case contains a string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span> denoting the numerator of the fraction. It is guaranteed that the length of the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span> is equal to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> and that it contains only lowercase English letters.</p><p>The third line of each test case contains a string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span> denoting the denominator of the fraction. It is guaranteed that the length of the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span> is equal to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> and that it contains only lowercase English letters.</p>\n<h2>Output</h2>\n<p>For each test case, print \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">YES</span>\" if you can simplify the fraction so that the resulting expression is exactly equal to the string <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"monospace\">six</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\texttt{six}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">NO</span>\" otherwise.</p><p>You can output \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">YES</span>\" and \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">NO</span>\" in any case (for example, strings \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">yEs</span>\", \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">yes</span>\" and \"<span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">Yes</span>\" will be recognized as a positive response).</p>\n<h2>Examples</h2>\n<div class=\"section-title\">Example</div><div class=\"sample-test\"><div class=\"input\"><div class=\"title\">Input</div><pre><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-0\">5</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-1\">4 1</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-1\">sinx</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-1\">n</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-2\">6 7</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-2\">xixsms</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-2\">mixsxim</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-3\">8 5</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-3\">fixtures</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-3\">frute</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-4\">3 3</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-4\">abc</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-4\">def</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-5\">5 10</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-5\">sixty</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-5\">codeforces</div></pre></div><div class=\"output\"><div class=\"title\">Output</div><pre><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-1\">YES</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-2\">NO</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-3\">YES</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-even test-example-line-4\">NO</div><div class=\"test-example-line test-example-line-odd test-example-line-5\">NO</div></pre></div></div>\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>In the first test case, the variable <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">n</span> appears in both the numerator and the denominator, so it can be cancelled. The remaining variables in the numerator are <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">s</span>, <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">i</span>, and <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">x</span>, which can be reordered to obtain <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">six</span>.</p><p>In the second test case, after cancelling all possible common variables, the numerator contains only <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">s</span> while the denominator contains only <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">mi</span>. Therefore, the fraction cannot be simplified to <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">six</span>.</p><p>In the third test case, the variables <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">f</span>, <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">r</span>, <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">u</span>, <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">t</span>, and <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">e</span> can be cancelled from both sides. The remaining variables in the numerator are <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">s</span>, <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">i</span>, and <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">x</span>, which can be reordered to form <span class=\"tex-font-style-tt\">six</span>.</p>",
